---
import Code from "#/components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "#/layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="embedded"
	title="Embedded SQL"
	lede="How squill finds SQL inside host code, what it will and won't rewrite, and how to point it somewhere new."
>
	<section id="built-ins">
		<h2>What gets formatted</h2>
		<p>
			An{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">
				<code>[[embedded]]</code> rule
			</a>{" "}
			tells squill which files hold SQL: it parses them with a tree-sitter
			grammar, runs an extraction query over the tree to find the strings
			holding SQL, and reformats those in place. The rest of the file is
			untouched &mdash; squill never reprints host code, only the string
			contents it was pointed at.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="squill.toml"
			code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.rs"]
grammar = "rust"`}
		/>
		<p>
			Built-in grammars, and what their default queries find. In a rule, each is
			named in lowercase: <code>rust</code>, <code>go</code>,{" "}
			<code>python</code>, <code>javascript</code>, <code>typescript</code>,{" "}
			<code>tsx</code>, <code>gleam</code>, <code>c++</code>, <code>c#</code>,{" "}
			<code>java</code>, <code>kotlin</code>, and <code>swift</code>.
		</p>
		<ul class="lang-list">
			<li>
				<img src="langs/rust.svg" alt="" />
				Rust: sqlx&rsquo;s <code class="boxed">query!</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query_as!</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query_scalar!</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query_unchecked!</code> macros and{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query</code>/<code class="boxed">query_as</code>/
				<code class="boxed">query_scalar</code> functions
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/go.svg" alt="" />
				Go: <code class="boxed">Query</code>,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">QueryRow</code>, <code class="boxed">Exec</code> and
				their <code class="boxed">Context</code> variants
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/python.svg" alt="" />
				Python: <code class="boxed">execute</code>-family methods and
				SQLAlchemy&rsquo;s <code class="boxed">text(...)</code>, with{" "}
				<code>%s</code> / <code>%(name)s</code> params preserved under{" "}
				<code>pyformat-params</code>
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/typescript.svg" alt="" />
				JavaScript, TypeScript, and TSX: <code class="boxed">.query</code>/
				<code class="boxed">.execute</code>/<code class="boxed">.prepare</code>{" "}
				calls and <code class="boxed">sql`&hellip;`</code> tagged templates
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/gleam.svg" alt="" />
				Gleam: <code class="boxed">sqlight</code> (as SQLite) and{" "}
				<code class="boxed">pog</code> (as the configured dialect)
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/cxx.svg" alt="" />
				C++: raw strings passed to <code class="boxed">sqlite3_prepare*</code>/
				<code class="boxed">sqlite3_exec</code> (as SQLite), libpq&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">PQexec</code>-family (as Postgres), and
				libpqxx-style <code class="boxed">exec</code>/
				<code class="boxed">query</code> methods
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/csharp.svg" alt="" />
				C#: raw strings passed to EF Core&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">migrationBuilder.Sql</code> and raw-SQL methods,
				Dapper&rsquo;s query/execute family, or assigned to{" "}
				<code class="boxed">CommandText</code>, with <code>@name</code> params
				preserved under <code>at-params</code>
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/java.svg" alt="" />
				Java: text blocks passed to JDBC, JPA, or Spring{" "}
				<code class="boxed">JdbcTemplate</code> calls, with JDBC&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code>?</code> placeholders preserved under <code>question-params</code>
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/kotlin.svg" alt="" />
				Kotlin: raw strings passed to JDBC, Spring{" "}
				<code class="boxed">JdbcTemplate</code>, or Exposed calls, with or
				without <code class="boxed">.trimIndent()</code>, and the same{" "}
				<code>question-params</code> for <code>?</code>
			</li>
			<li>
				<img src="langs/swift.svg" alt="" />
				Swift: <code>"""</code> strings passed to GRDB as its{" "}
				<code class="boxed">sql:</code> argument (as SQLite), or first to
				SQLite.swift&rsquo;s <code class="boxed">run</code>/
				<code class="boxed">execute</code>/<code class="boxed">prepare</code>/
				<code class="boxed">scalar</code>, PostgresNIO&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">query</code>, or SQLKit&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code class="boxed">raw</code>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The <a href="docs/recipes/rust/">recipes</a> show each of these at work,
			library by library, along with queries for the libraries the defaults
			don&rsquo;t cover.
		</p>
		<p>
			A file named explicitly on the command line that no rule covers, and that
			isn&rsquo;t named <code>*.sql</code>, doesn&rsquo;t format: squill stops
			and shows the rule to add.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="strings">
		<h2>Which strings are eligible</h2>
		<p>
			Only <em>multiline string syntaxes</em> are rewritten: Rust raw strings,
			Go backticks, Python triple quotes, JS template literals, Gleam strings,
			C++ and C# raw strings, Java text blocks, Kotlin raw strings, and Swift
			multi-line strings. The query is formatted just as it would be in a{" "}
			<code>.sql</code> file. A string written on one line (
			<code>R"(&hellip;)"</code>, <code>"""&hellip;"""</code>) stays on it while
			the query fits there; a string that already spans lines, or a query that
			doesn&rsquo;t fit, gets the quotes on lines of their own with the query
			between them.
		</p>
		<p>
			A plain Rust or Go string joins them once it already spans lines &mdash;
			written, escaped (<code>\n</code>), or continued. Its escapes come back
			exactly as they were spelled: squill reads them to read the SQL, and never
			respells one. When the SQL takes more than one line and every escape in it
			sat between SQL tokens, the string is rewritten as a raw string (
			<code>r#"&hellip;"#</code>, or backticks in Go); otherwise it keeps its
			own syntax, or, in Go, where a plain string can&rsquo;t span lines, is
			left alone. A single-line plain string without escapes stays
			byte-identical.
		</p>
		<p>
			That is also why there is no C grammar. A C string literal ends with its
			line, and SQL written over several lines in C is several literals side by
			side (<code>"SELECT id " "FROM t"</code>), so there is no single string to
			rewrite.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				A string with interpolation holes is formatted only where the library
				sends each hole as a parameter: postgres.js&rsquo;s and Bun&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code>sql`&hellip;`</code>, Prisma&rsquo;s <code>$queryRaw</code>, EF
				Core&rsquo;s <code>FromSql</code>, psycopg&rsquo;s t-strings,
				PostgresNIO&rsquo;s <code>query</code>, SQLKit&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code>\(bind: &hellip;)</code>, and GRDB&rsquo;s <code>literal:</code>.
				squill reads each hole as a parameter as wide as it is, and writes it
				back as it was. Everywhere else &mdash; Python f-strings, other JS
				templates, Kotlin&rsquo;s <code>$name</code>, a bare Swift{" "}
				<code>\(&hellip;)</code> &mdash; a hole pastes text into the SQL, and
				the string is left exactly as written.
			</p>
		</div>
		<h3>Safety</h3>
		<p>
			Every rewritten string passes the same safety check as a <code>.sql</code>{" "}
			file, and then the host file is parsed again: an edit that would add a
			syntax error, or whose string no longer reads back through the query
			exactly as written, is dropped. A string squill declines to rewrite
			&mdash; SQL that doesn&rsquo;t parse, an escape its query doesn&rsquo;t
			name, a result the string can&rsquo;t hold &mdash; is reported as a
			diagnostic with its line and column, and fails the run under{" "}
			<code>--strict</code>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="queries">
		<h2>Writing your own query</h2>
		<p>
			A rule&rsquo;s <code>query</code> replaces its grammar&rsquo;s default
			with a{" "}
			<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/using-parsers/queries/1-syntax.html">
				tree-sitter query
			</a>{" "}
			file, found relative to the config. A capture named <code>@sql</code>{" "}
			marks a string whose contents are SQL in the configured dialect.
		</p>
		<p id="dialects">
			A capture can name the dialect instead: <code>@sql.postgres</code> or{" "}
			<code>@sql.sqlite</code> reads that string in that dialect, whatever{" "}
			<code>dialect</code> says. It&rsquo;s for calls into a library that only
			speaks one &mdash; the built-in queries read sqlight&rsquo;s,
			GRDB&rsquo;s, and SQLite&rsquo;s C API&rsquo;s strings as SQLite, and
			libpq&rsquo;s as Postgres, so a project whose <code>dialect</code> is set
			for its other SQL still gets them right.{" "}
			<a href="docs/cli/#locate">
				<code>squill locate</code>
			</a>{" "}
			marks each string whose dialect came from the query, and a warning about
			one names the dialect it was read in. Any other <code>@sql.</code> suffix
			is an error.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="squill.toml"
			code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.rs"]
grammar = "rust"
query = ".config/squill/rust.scm"`}
		/>
		<Code
			lang="query"
			file=".config/squill/rust.scm"
			code={`((macro_invocation
   macro: (identifier) @_name
   (token_tree (raw_string_literal (string_content) @sql)))
 (#eq? @_name "my_sql")
 (#set! squill.raw)
 (#set! squill.multiline))`}
		/>
		<p>
			Captures whose names start with <code>_</code> are the usual tree-sitter
			convention for "match this, but it isn't the thing I want" &mdash; squill
			only rewrites <code>@sql</code> captures.{" "}
			<a href="docs/cli/#locate">
				<code>squill locate</code>
			</a>{" "}
			lists what a query captures, file by file, so you can check it before
			formatting anything.
		</p>
		<h3>Learning the query language</h3>
		<p>
			A query is a pattern over a file&rsquo;s syntax tree, so writing one
			starts with seeing that tree:
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Paste some code into the{" "}
				<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/7-playground.html">
					tree-sitter playground
				</a>{" "}
				to see its node and field names, and try a query live. For a grammar the
				playground doesn&rsquo;t have,{" "}
				<code class="boxed">tree-sitter parse file.ext</code> prints the same
				tree.
			</li>
			<li>
				Write the pattern: tree-sitter&rsquo;s docs cover the{" "}
				<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/using-parsers/queries/1-syntax.html">
					syntax
				</a>{" "}
				(nodes, fields, captures) and the{" "}
				<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/using-parsers/queries/2-operators.html">
					operators
				</a>{" "}
				&mdash; alternations like{" "}
				<code class="boxed">[(string) (raw_string)]</code>, and the{" "}
				<code class="boxed">.</code> anchor, which the default queries use to
				take only a call&rsquo;s first argument.
			</li>
			<li>
				Filter with{" "}
				<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/using-parsers/queries/3-predicates-and-directives.html">
					predicates
				</a>
				, within the three squill supports (below).
			</li>
			<li>
				Check it with{" "}
				<a href="docs/cli/#locate">
					<code class="boxed">squill locate</code>
				</a>
				. The <a href="docs/recipes/rust/">recipes</a> are worked examples, and
				the default queries are{" "}
				<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/tree/trunk/crates/embed/src/queries">
					in squill&rsquo;s source
				</a>
				.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<h3 id="properties">What a string allows</h3>
		<p>
			squill captures a string&rsquo;s text as written, and knows nothing about
			its syntax but what the query says &mdash; for every grammar, the built-in
			ones included. Unless the query says otherwise, it assumes the worst: a
			string is formatted only when it already spans lines (proof the syntax
			takes line breaks), and never when it holds a backslash (which might be an
			escape). A pattern can promise more with tree-sitter&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>#set!</code>:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<code>(#set! squill.raw)</code>: the string takes no escapes, so a
				backslash is just a backslash &mdash; Lua&rsquo;s{" "}
				<code>[[&hellip;]]</code>, Ruby&rsquo;s <code>&lt;&lt;~'SQL'</code>,
				Elixir&rsquo;s <code>~S</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>(#set! squill.multiline)</code>: the string takes raw line breaks,
				so one written on a single line can be given the multi-line layout.
				Promise it for a syntax meant for it, like Lua&rsquo;s long strings, and
				not for a language&rsquo;s everyday string.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>(#set! squill.escape "whitespace")</code>, once per kind: the
				escapes the string takes, which squill may read. It reads them only to
				read the SQL, and writes each back exactly as it was spelled, so an
				escape kind it isn&rsquo;t told about leaves the string alone rather
				than be misread. The kinds: <code>"whitespace"</code> (<code>\n</code>{" "}
				<code>\r</code> <code>\t</code>), <code>"punctuation"</code> (
				<code>\\</code> <code>\"</code> <code>\'</code>), <code>"\\$"</code>,{" "}
				<code>"\\`"</code>, <code>"\\xHH"</code>, <code>"\\uXXXX"</code>,{" "}
				<code>
					"\\u{"{"}XXXX{"}"}"
				</code>
				, <code>"\\UXXXXXXXX"</code>, <code>"\\NNN"</code> (octal, and so{" "}
				<code>\0</code>), <code>"line-continuation"</code>, and{" "}
				<code>"line-continuation-trim"</code> (Rust&rsquo;s, which drops the
				next line&rsquo;s indentation too). Where each escape is, the grammar
				says: its <code>escape_sequence</code> nodes count, and a grammar that
				names them otherwise can capture them as <code>@squill.escape</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>(#set! squill.promote-to-raw-syntax "r#\"{"{}"}\"#")</code>, once
				per form: raw syntaxes, tried in order, that a string spanning lines is
				rewritten in, its content where <code>{"{}"}</code> is. The first whose
				closing delimiter the SQL doesn&rsquo;t contain wins, and only when
				every escape in the string sat between SQL tokens, since a raw string
				can&rsquo;t spell one.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Where no one node spans a string&rsquo;s content &mdash; a Ruby
			heredoc&rsquo;s is a run of content and escape nodes &mdash; a quantifier
			can capture them all as one string, first to last:{" "}
			<code>[(heredoc_content) (escape_sequence)]+ @sql</code>.
		</p>
		<p>
			A capture named <code>@squill.parameter</code> marks a hole the library
			sends as a parameter, inside the SQL capture it falls in. squill reads it
			as a parameter exactly as wide as the hole, so the SQL lays out at its
			real width, and writes it back as it was. A hole can stand as a token of
			its own, or inside one (<code>'$&lbrace;x&rbrace;%'</code>); one right
			against SQL text (<code>$&lbrace;a&rbrace;$&lbrace;b&rbrace;</code>) or
			spanning lines is reported. One match&rsquo;s parameter captures make one
			hole, so Swift&rsquo;s <code>\(</code>, expression, and <code>)</code> can
			be captured together. SQL inside another string&rsquo;s hole (a
			postgres.js fragment) is part of that string&rsquo;s query, and left
			alone.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="query"
			file=".config/squill/postgres.scm"
			code={`((call_expression
   function: (identifier) @_tag
   arguments: (template_string (template_substitution) @squill.parameter))
 (#eq? @_tag "sql"))`}
		/>
		<p>
			And a capture named <code>@squill.skip</code> leaves out any SQL capture
			it overlaps: a hole in the string, like a JS template&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>$&lbrace;&hellip;&rbrace;</code>, or a region the string sits in,
			like a function you&rsquo;d rather squill didn&rsquo;t touch.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="query"
			file=".config/squill/skip.scm"
			code={`(template_substitution) @squill.skip

((function_declaration name: (identifier) @_name) @squill.skip
 (#eq? @_name "legacyMigration"))`}
		/>
		<Code
			lang="query"
			file=".config/squill/lua.scm"
			code={`((function_call
   name: (method_index_expression method: (identifier) @_m)
   arguments: (arguments . (string start: _ @_open content: (string_content) @sql)))
 (#eq? @_m "exec")
 (#any-of? @_open "[[" "[=[" "[==[")
 (#set! squill.raw)
 (#set! squill.multiline))`}
		/>
		<p>
			The built-in queries make their promises the same way; see them{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/tree/trunk/crates/embed/src/queries">
				in squill&rsquo;s source
			</a>
			. squill checks what it can. Under <code>squill.raw</code>, formatting
			must leave whatever follows each backslash exactly as it was, so a line
			continuation can&rsquo;t be moved. A false <code>squill.multiline</code>{" "}
			is caught by the re-parse only when the grammar rejects a line break in
			the string, and not every grammar does: Java&rsquo;s, Kotlin&rsquo;s, and
			Swift&rsquo;s accept one in a <code>"&hellip;"</code> string that the
			compiler wouldn&rsquo;t. Make that promise only for strings that keep it.
		</p>
		<h3>Supported predicates</h3>
		<p>
			<code>#eq?</code>, <code>#not-eq?</code>, and <code>#any-of?</code>{" "}
			&mdash; three of the{" "}
			<a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/using-parsers/queries/3-predicates-and-directives.html">
				predicates tree-sitter defines
			</a>
			. Regex predicates (<code>#match?</code>, <code>#not-match?</code>) are
			rejected up front, and so is any other custom predicate: a predicate the
			engine does not evaluate would silently widen your query to match things
			you never intended, so squill refuses the query instead of quietly
			over-matching.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="grammars">
		<h2>Bringing your own grammar</h2>
		<p>
			Any language with a tree-sitter grammar works: build it to wasm with{" "}
			<code>tree-sitter build --wasm</code> (many grammars publish one with each
			release) and point a rule at the file. The language name comes from the
			file name, the way that command writes it:{" "}
			<code>tree-sitter-lua.wasm</code> holds <code>lua</code>. A loaded grammar
			has no default query, so the rule needs one.
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="squill.toml"
			code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.lua"]
grammar = ".config/squill/tree-sitter-lua.wasm"
query = ".config/squill/lua.scm"
dialect = "sqlite"`}
		/>
		<Code
			lang="query"
			file=".config/squill/lua.scm"
			code={`((function_call
   name: (method_index_expression method: (identifier) @_m)
   arguments: (arguments . (string) @sql))
 (#eq? @_m "exec"))`}
		/>
		<p>
			Capture either the string&rsquo;s content node or the whole literal
			(squill takes the text between its opening and closing delimiter tokens).
			squill knows nothing about a loaded grammar&rsquo;s string syntax until
			the query{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#properties">says what its strings allow</a>.
		</p>
		<h3 id="urls">From a URL</h3>
		<p>
			A grammar can also come straight from where it&rsquo;s published, as an{" "}
			<code>https</code> URL naming the <code>.wasm</code> file:
		</p>
		<Code
			lang="toml"
			file="squill.toml"
			code={`[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.lua"]
grammar = "https://github.com/tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-lua/releases/download/v0.5.0/tree-sitter-lua.wasm"
query = ".config/squill/lua.scm"`}
		/>
		<p>
			The first time squill downloads it, it records the grammar&rsquo;s SHA-256
			in a <code>squill.lock</code> beside the config; commit that file. Every
			later download must match it, or squill stops and shows both hashes.
			Downloads are cached by hash in your user cache directory (
			<code>$XDG_CACHE_HOME/squill</code>, or the platform&rsquo;s own), so a
			locked grammar that&rsquo;s already cached never touches the network. In
			CI, <code>squill fmt --locked</code> refuses to record anything new: a
			grammar URL missing from <code>squill.lock</code> is an error instead of a
			download trusted on sight.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="indentation">
		<h2>Indentation</h2>
		<p>
			Embedded SQL is anchored to the host statement&rsquo;s indentation, and by
			default indents the way the file around it does: the same character, so a
			spaces-indented file never gains tabs, and the same step, so a file
			indented by four spaces gets four-space SQL. Set <code>indent</code> or{" "}
			<code>indent-width</code> in the{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rule</a> (or at the top level) to
			choose deliberately instead. Lines inside a SQL string that itself spans
			lines are data, and are never re-indented. A closing delimiter at the
			margin stays there, since some syntaxes need it there: a bare Ruby
			heredoc&rsquo;s terminator only ends it at the start of a line.
		</p>
		<p>
			<code>max-width</code> counts from the left edge of the file, not from the
			anchor, so a query nested deep in a function breaks sooner than the same
			query at the top level &mdash; though it always gets at least half the
			width to itself.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="semicolons">
		<h2>Semicolons</h2>
		<p>
			A query in host code usually goes without a final <code>;</code>, so
			embedded SQL loses its last one, where a <code>.sql</code> file gains one.
			Only the last: the <code>;</code> between statements in a multi-statement
			string are what separate them. Set{" "}
			<code>trailing-semicolons = "always"</code> in the rule to keep them
			instead.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
